# Labelled adverse-media relevance cases. Scored by eval/run_eval.py::score_adverse_media_relevance
# through the SHIPPED `finding_names_subject` predicate.
#
# Why this file exists. On 2026-08-26 a paired demonstration asked for adverse media on a
# fictional company. The deployment's grounded web search returned a REAL money-laundering
# prosecution naming real banks, marked it critical, and the risk policy turned that severity
# into a PROHIBITED band for a company the article never mentions. Two things were wrong and only
# one of them is about search quality: a returned article carried its severity straight into the
# most consequential field in the dossier, with nothing deterministic in between.
#
# The fix is `finding_names_subject`, and it was unit-tested and never SCORED. A property this
# consequential belongs in the gate, because a promotion that regressed it should not be
# certifiable, and because a unit test can be deleted in the same commit as the thing it guards.
#
# `keep` is a reviewer's answer to "is this article about this subject", written by reading it.
# The gate is deliberately conservative: dropping an unverifiable hit costs a finding, keeping
# one costs a subject the most severe band the system can assign, on evidence about somebody
# else. Subjects are fictional; the two real-world headlines are kept verbatim as the regression
# they are, and neither names any fictional subject.
{"id": "rel-real-scandal", "subject": "Meridian Harbour Holdings Pte Ltd", "headline": "Singapore's banking giants entangled in US$740 million money-laundering scandal", "snippet": "Some of the biggest local and international banks in Singapore are becoming embroiled in one of the city state's largest money laundering cases.", "keep": false}
{"id": "rel-named-in-full", "subject": "Meridian Harbour Holdings Pte Ltd", "headline": "Meridian Harbour Holdings Pte Ltd charged with fraud", "snippet": "", "keep": true}
